    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d509">

                <pb facs="0778" n="745" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v23/pg745"/>

                <head>509. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in the Congo<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d509-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1967-07-07T12:19:00-04:00">July 7, 1967,
                            12:19 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>2402. Ref: Kinshasa 135.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d509-f2"/> Please deliver following
                    reply to Mobutu’s letter of July 5 to President:</p>
                <p>“Dear Mr. President:</p>
                <p>I have received your letter of July 5 about the situation in the Democratic
                    Republic of the Congo. I am consulting urgently with my advisers here to
                    determine how, within the limitations of our own resources, <pb facs="0779"
                        n="746" xml:id="pg746"/> we can help your government at this difficult time.
                    We shall communicate with you as soon as possible through my Ambassador to the
                    Congo, Robert McBride. We are confident you will succeed in restoring order and
                    resuming the path on which you have already embarked toward stabilization and
                    progress in the Congo. The Congolese National Army, backed by the Congolese
                    people, acting together under your courageous leadership will, I am sure,
                    reestablish the calm necessary to that task.</p>
                <p>Sincerely, Lyndon B. Johnson”</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>Rusk</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d509-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d509-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files 1967–69, POL 23–9 THE CONGO.
                    Confidential; Immediate; Limdis. Drafted by Schaufele; cleared by Fredericks,
                    Walt Rostow, and Thompson of S/S; and approved by Eugene Rostow. Acting
                    Secretary of Defense Nitze was informed. Also sent to Brussels.</p>
            </note>
